WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.260
Hands down, if you only got one thing that you're going to do,

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.590
it has to be home visits.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:14.040
Home visits by far just makes it so that you have a more trusting relationship,

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.190
you have a good foundation to build a relationship off of,

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.700
and you can move forward from there in many different ways.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:23.700
My teachers are focused on one-on-ones at home-- go into the community,

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:26.840
meet your families, talk to them about your hopes and dreams.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.180
Just ask them, what do they want for their kids?

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.224
And parents usually don't hear that question,

00:00:32.223 --> 00:00:34.140
and they definitely don't hear it from anybody

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.300
that has dc.gov at the end of their email address.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.990
We assume we know how parents get information.

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:42.725
And through home visits, I know now how parents get information,

00:00:42.725 --> 00:00:44.580
and it's not always through a newsletter,

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.040
and it's not always through a calendar.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.880
That first year, I remember trying to do home visits was like pulling teeth.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.200
We were tracking parents down, and they did not want us to come to their house.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.860
You know, they saw us as, why do you want to come to the house?

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:56.360
What are they looking for, you know?

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:57.300
They didn't trust us.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.040
I was against it at first, until they explained

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.460
that we're not here to call CPS.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:06.972
We just want to get to know the child, what you expect from us,

00:01:06.972 --> 00:01:08.138
and what we expect from you.

00:01:08.138 --> 00:01:10.910
I said, well, why can't we do that at school?

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:14.650
And they said, well, this is something new that we're trying.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:17.510
But when they had it, I enjoyed it.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:23.100
It's to get to know the teacher on a personal level,

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.075
to find out his background or her background, or what school she went to,

00:01:27.075 --> 00:01:30.700
or how she got her education, or how he got his education.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:31.980
So I loved it.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.515
Now they're looking for me.

00:01:34.515 --> 00:01:35.890
Where's my home visit, Ms. McKay?

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:36.723
When are you coming?

00:01:36.723 --> 00:01:38.200
When are you coming to the house?

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.440
It's just a very different feel, and I think

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.780
a lot of that was just establishing that trust with parents.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:48.062
I went to one of the communities this past August

00:01:48.062 --> 00:01:49.270
to do some of my home visits.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:52.260
And I brought a newer staff member with me, who was a little nervous

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:53.468
and had never done it before.

00:01:53.468 --> 00:01:56.337
And, you know, it's a little bit of an area

00:01:56.337 --> 00:01:58.420
that you might not feel comfortable in necessarily

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.530
by yourself if you hadn't been there before.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:03.100
And we're walking.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.680
We're walking, and this man's walking up to me.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.080
And she looks nervous.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.590
And he's like, you Ms. McKay, aren't you?

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:11.200
And I was like, yeah, I am.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.840
And he was like, you taught my nephew whatever, whatever.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:14.535
And I was like, oh yeah.

00:02:14.535 --> 00:02:16.110
You know, we're talking for a little bit.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.901
And then I'm walking through the courtyard,

00:02:17.901 --> 00:02:21.070
and I hear my name coming from different houses, because the kids know you.

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:21.810
They know your face.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.590
And even if you didn't teach them, you may

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:26.950
have taught their friend, or their brother, or their sister.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:28.540
I love going on home visits.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.240
I love engaging with the families.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.777
It's just you're sitting there, holding a baby on your lap,

00:02:32.777 --> 00:02:34.110
and talking about this and that.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.526
You know, they're offering you food, and it's just-- yeah,

00:02:36.526 --> 00:02:39.270
it's definitely worth it.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:43.502
The family visits, I love them because they ask questions like, you know,

00:02:43.502 --> 00:02:45.480
what are their strengths, their weaknesses,

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.150
and how can they help to make them stronger in the class

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:50.790
or in a certain subject.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:54.742
So it's really-- I love the home visits

00:02:54.742 --> 00:02:56.075
I'm going to come to your house.

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:56.750
I'm going to sit down.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:57.955
I'm going to drink your tea and I'm going

00:02:57.955 --> 00:03:00.330
to talk about your kid and their hopes and dreams, right?

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:02.996
And I'm going to listen to your expectations, because, you know,

00:03:02.996 --> 00:03:04.920
parents expect us to do stuff too as teachers.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.013
It doesn't just go we, we, we, we expect you, you, you, you

00:03:08.013 --> 00:03:09.179
to do all this stuff, right?

00:03:09.179 --> 00:03:11.720
It's a two-way street.

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:13.950
It gives you more of a stake in that family.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.880
And because you're invested in them, they're

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:17.874
going to be more invested in you.

00:03:17.874 --> 00:03:19.540
That in itself was very new for parents.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.346
And they're like, oh, we like that.

00:03:21.346 --> 00:03:23.470
Somebody actually asking me what I want for my kid?

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:26.290
I feel like I'm an equal partner in this relationship.

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:27.390
Like, oh, I like that.

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:29.790
Ms. Bryant goes around there, honey.

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:32.880
She goes to them kids house she ain't scared of nothing or nobody.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.130
Ms. Bryant walks the neighborhood.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:39.240
She comes around there, she played with the kids, she jumped rope,

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.010
she goes to the ice cream truck.

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.740
She do it all for them kids.

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:47.910
It was very interesting to just sit there, and just lay back, and just

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:51.140
have a-- like it's your brother, or your,

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:52.890
sister, or your family member just sitting

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:54.730
there holding a whole conversation.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:58.180
And to be honest with you, they felt like they were at home.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:03.390
So when you have a person that feel like they at home, it's the best thing.

